<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Sakala</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1224 CE (approx.) · capital Lehola</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Estonian county around Viljandi, homeland of the elder Lembitu, who led the most determined resistance to the Livonian crusade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a>, in south-western central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a>, was one of the major ancient Estonian lands, with hillforts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viljandi">Viljandi</a> and Lehola. It bordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a> to the south, with whom it traded and fought, and appears in detail in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-chronicle-of-henry">Livonian Chronicle of Henry</a> from the early 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like other Estonian counties, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a> was led by elders who commanded the levies of its parishes and negotiated with neighbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lembitu">Lembitu of Lehola</a> organised alliances across <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a> and sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> against the Germans, an unusually wide coalition for the time.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lembitu">Lembitu</a> was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-st-matthews-day">Battle of St Matthew's Day</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viljandi">Viljandi</a> in 1217, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a> again submitted to the crusaders. An uprising in 1223 briefly expelled them, but Sakala was reconquered by 1224 and passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>, later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a>, which built Fellin castle there.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 800 CE – 1224 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.62%;width:8.44%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1224 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lehola</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lembitu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Estonian paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Estonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saccalia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=sakala" aria-label="Sakala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5193 185 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sakala lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5513 441h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5513 441h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lehola. <a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=sakala">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

